{
  "requirement": "No prompt, tool result, or tool error the harness gives the model MUST state a\nline number or line range of a project source, except within source text it\nquotes and where a tool error repeats a line number the model supplied. The\nharness MUST identify a source location by file and outline selector under\n{cite}`requirements.references.outline_selectors` instead.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.authoring.no_line_numbers`. I found no unacknowledged gap.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text**\n1. Tool results must not state a line number or range of a project source. Quoted source text is exempt.\n2. Tool errors must not state such a line number. The exception is an error that repeats a line number the model supplied.\n3. Prompts must not state such a line number. This covers the subject, site and citation messages and the mandatory inputs.\n4. The harness must identify locations by file and outline selector instead.\n\n**Test mapping**\n- **Habeas read results.**\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[reading_records_only_delivered_sources_and_their_terms]` asserts that item reads and line-range reads carry no `line_start` or `line_end`. They give the `item` selector instead. Recorded inputs are exactly file, selector and hash.\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[line_range_reads_record_the_innermost_covering_nodes]` makes the same assertions for Rust files and for plain-text `line@hash` selectors.\n- **Search results.**\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[search_merges_context_windows_and_respects_ignore_rules]` asserts that entries have no `line_start` and lines have no `line` key.\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[separate_windows_in_one_node_are_divided_by_an_elision_line]` asserts the exact entry shape of file, item, kind, tag and lines. It uses an elision line, not numbers, to separate windows.\n  - `ast_grep` shares `SearchNodes::entries` with `ripgrep`, so these tests cover it. I did not find an `ast_grep` test marked for this requirement.\n- **Outline.** `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs]` asserts the exact selector, kind, name and children shape. It also asserts there is no `line_start` on plain-text entries.\n- **References.** `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` asserts exact equality of the site objects, which have name, role, file and item only.\n- **Tool errors.**\n  - `src/authoring/line_numbers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_drop_the_source_line_of_a_parse_error]` covers parse errors with and without `line N:`. It also covers `file:N:` diagnostics, `first declared at file:N`, and YAML or TOML `at line N, column M`. Each is rewritten to the `file#` selector form. Both supplied forms, `file:N` and `file:N-M`, are preserved when the error says \"is not an inclusive range\", which is the stated exception.\n  - `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_give_the_model_no_line_numbers_it_did_not_supply]` is the end-to-end check. A read past the end repeats only the supplied `99`. A Rust parse error does contain `line N: ` when produced by `tools::dispatch`. The request actually delivered to the provider by `assess` contains \"cannot parse\" and does not match `line \\d+: `.\n- **Prompts.**\n  - `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[site_reviews_use_their_prompt_and_record_applies_and_the_site]` checks the site subject message against the filled template. The message carries the file, the selector and `matches_value()` output.\n  - `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[sites_are_the_innermost_nodes_of_site_matches_and_violations_within_them]` asserts `matches_value()` equals `{kind, query, item, text}` exactly, with no lines.\n  - `src/authoring/inputs.rs#mod[tests]/fn[anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs]` asserts the mandatory inputs are exactly `{file, item, hash, kind, text}`, with no line fields. This covers `to_model_input`.\n  - `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[submission_description_tests]/fn[submission_descriptions_explain_arguments_and_successful_completion]` checks that the submission tool descriptions instruct \"outline selectors\" and \"never line numbers\".\n- **Selector grammar.** `requirements.references.outline_selectors` is cited context. The selector forms appear in the exact assertions above, such as `fn[work]`, `mod[inner]/fn[second]` and `line@…`.\n\n**Weaker areas, none a gap**\n- The citation-review payload test and the acknowledgments metadata tests are not marked `{test}` for this requirement. Their payloads (role, name, commentary; file, item, body, issues) carry no line fields. Nothing the text names is left unchecked.\n- No test scans entire prompts for numeric patterns. The dynamic prompt parts are instead pinned by exact-shape assertions, and the static prompt text contains no project line numbers.",
  "references": "5f07d59d3d70ca6a503110d2a797133c2b9f1eafd54fb53e7ee2fb02587ec51a",
